{% extends "race-page.njk" %}

{% block ending %}
	<p>From loop 60, only Makhlouf and Warren were left. On loops 60 to 62 they finished within two seconds of each other.</p>
	<p>Loop 63 split them. Makhlouf ran 45:25. Warren ran 52:33.</p>
	<p>Warren ran 54:59 on loop 64. Then 55:21 on loop 65, his slowest loop of the race. That left him 4 minutes 39 seconds of rest.</p>
	<p>He started loop 66 and did not finish it in time. Makhlouf ran it alone, in 47:21, and won.</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block dropouts %}
	<p>Four loops took far more runners than the loops next to them. Each one lines up with a distance every runner already knows: a marathon, 50 miles, 100 km and 100 miles.</p>
	<p>100 miles took 17. That may not sound like a lot. But only 64 runners got that far, so more than one in four stopped right there.</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block women %}
	<p>On loop 1, the women's middle time was 48:59. The men's was 48:58. One second apart.</p>
	<p>From loop 2 to loop 18, the women's middle time was slower on every loop. On 12 of those 17 loops, it was slower by a minute or more.</p>
	<p>Loop 19 flipped it. Eleven women finished that loop. Their middle time was 19 seconds faster than the men's.</p>
	<p>The second morning began with loop 25. Only five women were still running. From there, their middle time was faster than the men's on ten loops in a row.</p>
{% endblock %}

{% block daynight %}
	<p>The first day took out more runners than the first night: 348 against 217.</p>
	<p>Across the whole race, day loops took almost twice as many runners as night loops. But there were almost twice as many day hours too. Spread the stops across the hours, and day and night come out close to even.</p>
{% endblock %}
